    },
    "grade": "9-10",
    "points": 5,
    "timeMinutes": null,
    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "The diagram shows a pentagon. Sepideh draws five circles with centres $A$, $B$, $C$, $D$, $E$ such that the two circles on each side of the pentagon touch. The lengths of the sides of the pentagon are given. Which point is the centre of the largest circle that she draws?",
    "image": "qimages/kangaroo/ca-2016-g0910/q27.webp",
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "$A$"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "$B$"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "$C$"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "$D$"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "$E$"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "A",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Solve for the radii",
        "body": "Let the radii be $a, b, c, d, e$. Touching circles on a side means the side equals the sum of the two radii:\n$$a + b = 16,\\quad b + c = 14,\\quad c + d = 17,\\quad d + e = 13,\\quad e + a = 14.$$\nAdding all five: $2(a + b + c + d + e) = 74$, so the sum is $37$. Then $a = 37 - (b + c) - (d + e) = 37 - 14 - 13 = 10$, and $b = 6$, $c = 8$, $d = 9$, $e = 4$. The largest circle is centred at $A$.\n\n**Answer: (A) $A$**"
      }
    ],
    "topics": [
      "algebra",
      "geometry"
    ],
    "difficulty": 3,
    "duplicateOf": null,
    "reviewNote": null
  },
  {
